{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Stakeholders in School AI","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting stakeholders in school ai to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"03","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/02"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_calibration","cat531-supplement_04_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-03":["cat531-supplement_04_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Stakeholders in School AI\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Stakeholders In School AI, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-03-stakeholders-in-school-ai.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Stakeholders in School AI: Stakeholders In School AI, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery school AI tool is purchased by someone and used on someone, and those are rarely the same person. A district administrator signs the contract, a teacher runs the tool, and a student, often the one with the least voice in the room, absorbs whatever the tool gets wrong. Stakeholder analysis is the discipline of naming everyone with something at stake before the decision is made, especially the parties whose interests are structurally easy to overlook. Skip it and you will design for the people who are present in the meeting, which is exactly the wrong set.\n\nThe move that separates real analysis from a polite list is finding the silent stakeholder: the person most affected by the tool who is least likely to be consulted about it. In school AI this is almost always a student in a marginalized position, an English learner, a student with a disability, a student whose data is most sensitive. A stakeholder map that surfaces that person and asks whose interest the design currently centers is a defensible artifact; a map that lists \"students, teachers, parents\" and stops is decoration.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- List the full stakeholder set for a school AI proposal, distinguishing buyers, operators, subjects, and gatekeepers (the data officer, the vendor).\n- Name each stakeholder's actual interest, including conflicts (the vendor wants adoption and data; parents want privacy).\n- Identify the silent stakeholder: the most-affected party least likely to be in the decision.\n- State whose interest the current design centers, and propose a move that re-centers it toward the most-affected student.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA school plans to adopt an AI writing-feedback tutor that grades and comments on essays automatically. In the adoption meeting are the principal, two department heads, and the vendor's sales engineer. Everyone in the room benefits from \"yes\": the principal gets a modernization win, the teachers get time back, the vendor gets a contract and a stream of student writing to train on. The students whose essays the model will misread, particularly English learners whose phrasing the grammar engine flags as error rather than dialect, are not in the room and will not be asked. The whole point of a stakeholder map is to put that absent student on the page before the decision hardens.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA stakeholder analysis for school AI maps every party with an interest in the tool, makes their conflicting interests explicit, and then asks the uncomfortable question of whose interest the design actually serves. Three distinctions keep the map honest.\n\nFirst, separate roles. A single tool has buyers (who decide), operators (who run it), subjects (whom it acts on), and gatekeepers (who must approve, like a district data officer). These roles have different power and different exposure, and conflating them hides the imbalance.\n\nSecond, name interests as they actually are, including the ones nobody says aloud. The vendor's interest is adoption and access to student data; that is not sinister, but it is not neutral, and a map that lists the vendor as \"provides the tool\" has erased the part that matters.\n\n**Legal anchor.** The vendor's data interest and the parent-consent move both have statutory hooks, not just ethical ones: FERPA governs disclosure of student education records and the parental-consent requirement behind any consent step, and COPPA governs data collection from students under 13. The FTC's 2025 COPPA amendments tighten this further, limiting data retention and requiring separate verifiable parental consent before children's data is disclosed to third parties, including for model training, as of 2026.\n\nThird, find the silent stakeholder by asking: who is most affected and least consulted? In school AI the answer correlates almost perfectly with who has the least institutional power. The English learner whose syntax the model scores as error, the student in special education whose accommodations the tool ignores, the family without the literacy to read a consent form, these are the people the design will fail quietly.\n\nThe course anchors do pointed work. **Critical edtech studies** is the lens that insists you ask who benefits and who is burdened, rather than assuming a tool that helps \"students\" helps all students equally. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** names the vendor's data interest as a stake rather than a service. **Teacher professional judgment** is what lets you, the operator who actually sees the students, speak for the silent stakeholder in a room where no one else will.\n\n**Recent evidence.** The English-learner concern is documented, not speculative: peer-reviewed work has directly investigated bias in automated essay scores for elementary-age English learners (Wilson & Huang, 2024), and a systematic review of automated essay scoring reports recurring construct-irrelevant variance and fairness limitations across systems (Ramesh & Sanampudi, 2022), supporting the claim that grammar models can score non-standard phrasing as error rather than dialect or developmental form.\n\n## Worked Example: Mapping Stakeholders Around an AI Tutoring Pilot\n\nA school plans to adopt an AI writing-feedback tutor. Before deciding, a teacher maps who has a stake and what each one stands to gain or lose. Work it as numbered judgment calls.\n\nThe proposed pilot: a vendor AI grades and comments on student essays automatically.\n\nStep-by-step stakeholder map:\n\n- Step 1. List the stakeholders, not just the buyers. Students, parents, teachers, the vendor, the district data officer, and English-learner support staff.\n- Step 2. Name each one's interest. Students want faster feedback; teachers want time back; the vendor wants adoption and data; parents want privacy; the data officer wants compliance; EL staff want feedback that does not penalize non-standard syntax.\n- Step 3. Find the silent stakeholder. English learners are affected most yet are rarely consulted; the AI may score their phrasing as error rather than dialect or developmental form.\n- Step 4. Decide whose interest the design currently centers. Right now the design centers teacher time and vendor adoption, not the most-affected students.\n\nBefore (weak): \"Teachers and students are the stakeholders, and the tool helps both.\" This lists only the visible, aligned parties.\n\nAfter (strong): \"Six stakeholders, with conflicting interests. The silent stakeholder is English learners, whose phrasing the AI may misread as error. The current design centers teacher time and vendor adoption. Negotiated move: pilot only with a teacher override on EL essays and a parent consent step. Evidence for the EL concern = the vendor's grammar model has no dialect setting.\"\n\nThe strong map names interests, surfaces the silent stakeholder, and states whose interest the design centers, which a colleague can audit. (The EL concern cited as evidence is itself evidence-backed: see Wilson & Huang, 2024, on automated essay-score bias for English learners.)\n\nYou are the one teacher in the adoption meeting who sees the students daily. Decide what you say.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Speaking for the silent stakeholder\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"In the room: principal, two department heads, the vendor sales engineer. The AI writing-feedback tutor would grade and comment on essays automatically. Everyone present benefits from a yes. Your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Agree it helps students and teachers and move to approve\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"You mapped only the aligned, present parties. That is a list of people who like the tool, not a stakeholder analysis.\"},{\"label\":\"Ask who is most affected by the tool yet not in this room\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This is the move that finds the silent stakeholder: the most-affected party least likely to be consulted.\"},{\"label\":\"Defer entirely to the vendor on accuracy claims\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"The vendor has an adoption-and-data interest; treating its claim as neutral erases the stake that matters most.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: whose interest does a map of only-aligned parties protect by omission?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The students the tool will fail quietly, who are absent\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Name the absent, most-affected party before the decision hardens.\"}],\"feedback\":\"A map with no conflict is decoration.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You identify English learners as the silent stakeholder: the grammar model may score their phrasing as error rather than dialect or developmental form. Whose interest does the current design center?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The most-affected students; the design already protects them\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Not yet. Right now the design centers teacher time and vendor adoption, not the EL student.\"},{\"label\":\"Teacher time and vendor adoption, not the most-affected students\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Correct, and naming that honestly is what lets you propose a re-centering move.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor: what is the vendor’s actual interest?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adoption and access to student writing to train on\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Name it as a stake, then return to finding the silent stakeholder.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Check the EL student against the design: is there a dialect setting in the grammar model?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; so the design currently penalizes developmental syntax\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The evidence is the missing dialect setting.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Propose one concrete re-centering move with evidence, not a hope.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"Pilot only with a required teacher override on EL essays plus a parent consent step, because the vendor model has no dialect setting\\\"\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"This is a re-centering move a colleague can audit: it names the protected party, the mechanism, and the evidence.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"Trust teachers to be fair when they read the AI feedback\\\"\",\"next\":\"n7\",\"feedback\":\"A hope is not a mechanism. Re-centering needs a concrete move, not goodwill.\"}]},\"n7\":{\"prompt\":\"Convert the hope into a mechanism.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Require a teacher override on flagged EL essays before any score stands\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Now the protection is structural, not dependent on individual fairness.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You re-centered the design on English learners with a teacher override and named the evidence.\",\"feedback\":\"Stakeholder analysis is finished only when it surfaces a silent stakeholder and proposes a concrete, evidence-backed move that shifts the center toward them.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore approving any school AI tool, work the map in this order:\n\n1. **Roster everyone:** buyers, operators, subjects, gatekeepers, and the vendor, each as a distinct line.\n2. **Name interests honestly,** including the data and adoption interests no one volunteers.\n3. **Find the silent stakeholder** by asking who is most affected and least consulted.\n4. **Re-center:** state whose interest the design currently serves and one concrete move that shifts it toward the most-affected student.\n\nIf your map has no silent stakeholder, you have not finished; you have listed the people who were already in the room.\n\nPut the four map moves in the order the decision rule requires.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Roster everyone: buyers, operators, subjects, gatekeepers, vendor\", \"Name each interest honestly, including unspoken data and adoption interests\", \"Find the silent stakeholder: most affected and least consulted\", \"Re-center: name whose interest the design serves and one move that shifts it\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A stakeholder map for an attendance-prediction tool lists 'district, counselors, students, data officer, vendor' and stops there. Why is this map not yet finished?\" explanation=\"The map rosters parties but never asks who is most affected and least consulted. The flagged student, scored without consent and possibly tracked into lowered expectations, is the silent stakeholder, and a finished map surfaces that person and proposes a re-centering move.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is finished; all the parties are listed\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It never surfaces a silent stakeholder or a re-centering move\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It should list more vendors for comparison\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It needs a more detailed cost estimate\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating \"stakeholders\" as a synonym for \"people who like the tool,\" which produces a map of aligned interests and zero tension. The second is writing the analysis after the decision, as a justification, rather than before it, as a check. A map built to defend a choice already made will conveniently never find a silent stakeholder, because finding one would reopen the question.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply it to an attendance-prediction tool that flags students \"at risk of dropping out.\" Roster the parties: district (buyer), counselors (operators), students (subjects), the data officer (gatekeeper), the vendor. Name interests: the district wants a measurable intervention metric; counselors want early warning; the vendor wants the predictive contract and the historical data to refine its model. Now find the silent stakeholder: the flagged student, who never consented to being scored and may be tracked into lowered expectations by a label they cannot see or contest. Whose interest does the design center? The district's metric and the vendor's model, not the student's right to be treated as more than a risk score. The re-centering move: make every flag generate a private counselor conversation, never an automated placement, and give families the right to know and contest the label. The mechanics are identical to the writing-tutor case; only the silent stakeholder's specific harm changes. This harm is documented, not hypothetical: Wisconsin's Dropout Early Warning System was found to raise false alarms about Black and Hispanic students at significantly higher rates than white students, to be wrong about three-quarters of the time when predicting non-graduation, and to attach color-coded \"high risk\" labels students could not see or contest (The Markup, 2023; see also Yu et al., 2021, on fairness audits of dropout-prediction models).\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: each stakeholder on the left, their actual (not stated) interest on the right, and circle the row for the party who is most affected and least present. A strong reader can answer \"whose interest does this design center right now\" with a name, not a hope, and can propose one move that shifts the center. If your note lists only parties whose interests align, you have not found the conflict that makes the decision a judgment.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong stakeholder map names roles distinctly, states interests including the unspoken ones, surfaces a silent stakeholder, and proposes a concrete re-centering move with evidence. Tidy formatting is the last layer. If the map has no conflicting interests, mark the gap and ask BigAL to help you locate the party your list is protecting by omission rather than for a general explanation of stakeholders.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"The design currently centers teacher time and vendor adoption; I re-centered it on English learners with a required teacher override on flagged EL essays, because the vendor's model has no dialect setting and would otherwise penalize developmental syntax as error.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The transfer case warns that wanting to adopt a tool makes you a biased mapper. Explain why running a stakeholder analysis on a tool you personally like is the real test of whether you understand the method.\" answer=\"When you want the yes, your incentive is to map only the aligned, present parties and to not look too hard for a silent stakeholder, because finding one would reopen a decision you have already made in your head. Running the analysis honestly against your own preference means deliberately asking who is most affected and least consulted even when the answer is inconvenient. If you only find silent stakeholders on tools you already distrust, the analysis is a ritual that confirms what you wanted; if you find them on tools you like, it is a method you actually run.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the analysis to a tool you personally like and want to adopt: a discussion-prompt generator you find genuinely useful. The challenge is that wanting the \"yes\" makes you a biased mapper, prone to not looking too hard for a silent stakeholder. Working this version honestly tests whether stakeholder analysis is a method you run even against your own preference, or a ritual you perform only on tools you already distrust.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-03-stakeholders-in-school-ai-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n(These support moves map onto the misconception-rail actions this section uses: *ask* drives the question move, *explain* drives both the explanation and the weak/strong comparison, and *represent* corresponds to showing the idea in a new form, as the transfer and connection moves do.)\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 04.03. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how surfacing the silent stakeholder reshapes the design and drives evidence-based revision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2014). Distrusting educational technology: Critical questions for changing times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Wilson, J., & Huang, Y. (2024). Validity of automated essay scores for elementary-age English language learners: Evidence of bias? *Assessing Writing, 60*. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2024.100815\n- Ramesh, D., & Sanampudi, S. K. (2022). An automated essay scoring systems: A systematic literature review. *Artificial Intelligence Review, 55*. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10462-021-10068-2\n- Feathers, T. (2023). False alarm: How Wisconsin uses race and income to label students \"high risk.\" The Markup. https://themarkup.org/machine-learning/2023/04/27/false-alarm-how-wisconsin-uses-race-and-income-to-label-students-high-risk\n- Yu, R., et al. (2021). Should college dropout prediction models include protected attributes? *Proceedings of the Eighth ACM Conference on Learning @ Scale*. https://doi.org/10.1145/3430895.3460139\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). FTC finalizes changes to children's privacy rule (amended COPPA Rule). https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/01/ftc-finalizes-changes-childrens-privacy-rule-limiting-companies-ability-monetize-kids-data\n- U.S. Department of Education. FERPA (educational records and parental consent for disclosure). https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/ferpa\n- Sperling, K., et al. (2024). In search of artificial intelligence (AI) literacy in teacher education: A scoping review. *Computers and Education Open, 6*. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.caeo.2024.100169\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","stem":"A teacher's stakeholder map for an AI essay-feedback pilot lists only 'students and teachers.' Which addition most improves the map?","options":["A nicer title and a legend.","The English learners whose phrasing the AI may misread, since they are the most affected yet least consulted group.","The room number where the pilot runs.","A second copy of the teacher entry."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A stakeholder map must surface the silent, most-affected party, not just the visible users.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_only_visible_stakeholders","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","stem":"A map states that the AI tutor 'helps everyone involved.' Why is this claim weak as stakeholder analysis?","options":["It is grammatically incorrect.","It hides real conflicts of interest, such as vendor data goals versus student privacy versus teacher time.","It does not mention the price of the tool.","It uses the word everyone, which is informal."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Stakeholder analysis exists to surface conflicting interests, not to declare universal benefit.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_aligned_interests_assumption","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","stem":"Which stakeholder is a teacher most likely to leave off the map, even though its interests can shape the tool's design?","options":["The students in the class.","The AI vendor, whose interest in adoption and data collection can drive design choices.","The classroom teacher.","The principal who approved the schedule."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The vendor is a stakeholder with data and adoption interests that often outweigh stated student benefits.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_vendor_neutral","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_stakeholders_in_school_ai_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"After mapping six stakeholders, which conclusion is best supported as a defensible judgment?","options":["The pilot is fine because most stakeholders benefit.","The current design centers teacher time and vendor adoption, so a teacher override on English-learner essays and a parent consent step are needed before piloting.","The pilot should be cancelled because vendors are untrustworthy.","Stakeholders will sort themselves out during the pilot."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong judgment names whose interest the design centers and proposes a concrete, evidence-linked adjustment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_only_visible_stakeholders","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_stakeholders_in_school_ai"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531-supplement_04_03_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-04-03-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_only_visible_stakeholders","pattern":"stakeholders_limited_to_buyers_and_users","description":"Listing only the obvious stakeholders (teachers, students, vendor) and missing the silent or most-affected parties","trigger":"learner names buyers and direct users but omits English learners, parents, or the data officer","feedback":"Ask who is most affected yet least consulted. The silent stakeholder, often the most-affected group, must appear on the map.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_aligned_interests_assumption","pattern":"assuming_stakeholder_interests_align","description":"Assuming all stakeholders want the same thing because the tool is described as helpful","trigger":"learner writes that a tool 'helps everyone' without naming any conflicting interest","feedback":"Name at least one genuine conflict. Vendor adoption, teacher time, and student equity often pull in different directions.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_3_vendor_neutral","pattern":"vendor_treated_as_neutral_provider","description":"Treating the AI vendor as a neutral service rather than a stakeholder with its own data and adoption interests","trigger":"learner leaves the vendor off the stakeholder map or lists it without an interest","feedback":"The vendor is a stakeholder. Name its interest in data and adoption, since that interest can shape the design more than student needs.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c06ccafcd74b8d308e331d5b90511facde872bada04d1d7eb6a75ec3578a8414","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}